1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,025
At twenty-seven minutes and fifty-three seconds past one in the morning, a duty

2
00:00:04,025 --> 00:00:06,827
officer in Baltimore gave an order to stop the traffic.

3
00:00:07,127 --> 00:00:11,222
Sixty-seven seconds later the bridge was in the river, and six men who had been

4
00:00:11,222 --> 00:00:12,932
filling potholes on it were gone.

5
00:00:13,232 --> 00:00:15,182
Everyone who could act, acted.

6
00:00:15,482 --> 00:00:17,897
So how did the sixty-seven seconds run out?

7
00:00:19,697 --> 00:00:23,830
There is a particular kind of American job that only exists after midnight.

8
00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:28,366
The road belongs to everybody during the day, so the work on it has to happen when

9
00:00:28,366 --> 00:00:29,192
nobody wants it.

10
00:00:29,492 --> 00:00:30,374
Cones.

11
00:00:30,674 --> 00:00:32,299
A truck with a light bar.

12
00:00:32,599 --> 00:00:36,972
A crew that knows each other's coffee order and works in the cold with the river

13
00:00:36,972 --> 00:00:37,847
underneath them.

14
00:00:38,147 --> 00:00:43,134
On the night of the twenty-fifth of March, 2024, that crew was on the Francis Scott

15
00:00:43,134 --> 00:00:45,717
Key Bridge in Baltimore, patching the deck.

16
00:00:46,017 --> 00:00:50,707
Eight people were up there — a seven-person road maintenance crew and one inspector.

17
00:00:51,007 --> 00:00:55,448
Below them, at the Seagirt Marine Terminal, a container ship was finishing her

18
00:00:55,448 --> 00:00:55,790
cargo.

19
00:00:56,090 --> 00:00:57,669
She was called the Dali.

20
00:00:57,969 --> 00:01:04,192
She flew the flag of Singapore, and her international number was 9697428.

21
00:01:04,492 --> 00:01:09,147
She had come to the United States on the nineteenth of March, worked Newark for two

22
00:01:09,147 --> 00:01:13,690
days, then Norfolk for one, and moored in Baltimore Harbor on the twenty-third at

23
00:01:13,690 --> 00:01:16,102
thirty-six minutes past two in the morning.

24
00:01:16,402 --> 00:01:21,371
She was a working ship on a working schedule, and she was bound for Sri Lanka.

25
00:01:21,671 --> 00:01:22,739
She was loaded.

26
00:01:23,039 --> 00:01:27,433
Not riding high and empty on her way to pick something up — loaded, which matters,

27
00:01:27,433 --> 00:01:31,452
because a loaded ship carries her momentum differently and answers a rudder

28
00:01:31,452 --> 00:01:32,095
differently.

29
00:01:32,395 --> 00:01:33,928
Two halves of one picture.

30
00:01:34,228 --> 00:01:36,736
The night lives in the distance between them.

31
00:01:37,036 --> 00:01:39,776
Up on the deck, the work is small and physical.

32
00:01:40,076 --> 00:01:42,862
Somebody is walking backwards guiding a truck.

33
00:01:43,162 --> 00:01:46,273
Somebody is checking that a cone has not blown over.

34
00:01:46,573 --> 00:01:51,293
It is the kind of job where the most dangerous thing you expect is a car that does

35
00:01:51,293 --> 00:01:52,099
not slow down.

36
00:01:52,399 --> 00:01:55,185
Down at the terminal, the work is enormous and invisible.

37
00:01:55,485 --> 00:01:59,200
A ship this size is a floating power station with cargo strapped to it.

38
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:02,333
She makes her own electricity from four diesel generators.

39
00:02:02,633 --> 00:02:04,909
She distributes it on a high-voltage bus.

40
00:02:05,209 --> 00:02:10,138
She steps it down through transformers to run everything a person would recognise as

41
00:02:10,138 --> 00:02:12,779
a ship — the lights, the pumps, the steering.

42
00:02:13,079 --> 00:02:15,029
Neither group can see the other's risks.

43
00:02:15,329 --> 00:02:17,047
That is not a criticism.

44
00:02:17,347 --> 00:02:19,344
That is just what a port is.

45
00:02:19,644 --> 00:02:23,870
And there was one more witness aboard that neither of them would have thought about.

46
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:29,312
International rules require a ship of the Dali's tonnage and age to carry a voyage

47
00:02:29,312 --> 00:02:32,761
data recorder — the maritime equivalent of a black box.

48
00:02:33,061 --> 00:02:36,933
Through everything that followed, with the ship's lighting gone twice, the bridge

49
00:02:36,933 --> 00:02:37,937
audio kept recording.

50
00:02:38,237 --> 00:02:40,141
Somebody would hear this night.

51
00:02:40,441 --> 00:02:44,761
At about a quarter to one, the senior pilot ordered the main engine to dead slow

52
00:02:44,761 --> 00:02:45,085
ahead.

53
00:02:45,385 --> 00:02:47,521
That engine was not a small thing.

54
00:02:47,821 --> 00:02:52,233
Fifty-five thousand six hundred and twenty-six horsepower, turning one propeller.

55
00:02:52,533 --> 00:02:54,809
Call it five hundred family cars.

56
00:02:55,109 --> 00:02:56,131
One shaft.

57
00:02:56,431 --> 00:02:57,546
One loaded ship.

58
00:02:57,846 --> 00:02:58,914
In the dark.

59
00:02:59,214 --> 00:03:03,719
At about seven minutes past one, once she was in the channel, the tugboats let go.

60
00:03:04,019 --> 00:03:05,180
That is normal.

61
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:06,548
Every night.

62
00:03:06,848 --> 00:03:07,823
Every port.

63
00:03:08,123 --> 00:03:10,863
From that moment she moved under her own power.

64
00:03:11,163 --> 00:03:12,370
Two pilots aboard.

65
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:14,899
A bridge she had passed under before.

66
00:03:15,199 --> 00:03:19,471
At about twenty-five minutes past one, she was six tenths of a mile out.

67
00:03:19,771 --> 00:03:21,211
Three of her own lengths.

68
00:03:21,511 --> 00:03:22,858
And the lights went out.

69
00:03:23,158 --> 00:03:27,802
Two electrical breakers — HR1 and LR1 — opened without being told to.

70
00:03:28,102 --> 00:03:32,467
In the space of that, the ship lost her lighting and most of her equipment.

71
00:03:32,767 --> 00:03:35,414
She lost the pumps that cooled the main engine.

72
00:03:35,714 --> 00:03:38,036
She lost the pumps that moved the steering gear.

73
00:03:38,336 --> 00:03:41,773
The emergency generator started and picked up the emergency bus.

74
00:03:42,073 --> 00:03:43,373
And the crew went to work.

75
00:03:43,673 --> 00:03:48,271
They closed HR1 and LR1 by hand, and the power came back to the whole vessel.

76
00:03:48,571 --> 00:03:52,983
At twenty-six minutes and thirty-nine seconds past one, the pilots called for a tug.

77
00:03:53,283 --> 00:03:56,209
The Eric McAllister answered immediately and turned toward them.

78
00:03:56,509 --> 00:03:57,949
She was three miles away.

79
00:03:58,249 --> 00:03:59,782
She never reached the ship.

80
00:04:00,082 --> 00:04:04,000
The pilots' dispatcher got on the phone to the Coast Guard and told them the Dali

81
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:04,726
had lost power.

82
00:04:05,026 --> 00:04:08,416
Then, two tenths of a mile from the bridge, it happened again.

83
00:04:08,716 --> 00:04:14,104
Two more breakers — DGR3 and DGR4 — opened, and this time the generators came off

84
00:04:14,104 --> 00:04:16,100
the high-voltage bus entirely.

85
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:18,583
Total loss of power, high side and low.

86
00:04:18,883 --> 00:04:22,883
At twenty-seven minutes and twenty-five seconds past one, a pilot picked up the

87
00:04:22,883 --> 00:04:25,060
radio and warned every vessel on the water.

88
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:29,455
Seven seconds after that, the duty officer in Baltimore gave his order about the

89
00:04:29,455 --> 00:04:29,865
traffic.

90
00:04:30,165 --> 00:04:34,323
And thirty-one seconds after the second blackout, the crew closed two different

91
00:04:34,323 --> 00:04:36,481
breakers by hand and got the lights back.

92
00:04:36,781 --> 00:04:39,846
They got the electricity back before the ship touched the bridge.

93
00:04:40,146 --> 00:04:42,236
They did not get the propeller back.

94
00:04:42,536 --> 00:04:43,929
One more thing was true.

95
00:04:44,229 --> 00:04:47,015
The emergency power could run a single steering pump.

96
00:04:47,315 --> 00:04:51,808
But that pump, running alone on emergency power, was designed to turn the rudder

97
00:04:51,808 --> 00:04:54,560
more slowly than it would with all pumps working.

98
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:59,476
And a rudder needs water moving past it to steer at all — so without the propeller

99
00:04:59,476 --> 00:05:02,290
turning, it would have been less effective anyway.

100
00:05:02,590 --> 00:05:03,937
The ship had a wheel.

101
00:05:04,237 --> 00:05:05,955
Turning it did almost nothing.

102
00:05:06,255 --> 00:05:11,085
At about twenty-nine minutes past one, the Dali struck pier number seventeen.

103
00:05:11,385 --> 00:05:12,964
The piers were not naked.

104
00:05:13,264 --> 00:05:18,212
They were protected by structures called dolphins — sheet pile and concrete, built

105
00:05:18,212 --> 00:05:20,927
to take a hit and keep a ship off the column.

106
00:05:21,227 --> 00:05:25,387
Two of them are drawn on the Safety Board's own map of the Dali's last three

107
00:05:25,387 --> 00:05:25,825
minutes.

108
00:05:26,125 --> 00:05:27,286
They were not enough.

109
00:05:27,586 --> 00:05:31,719
Why they were not enough is still standing over other people's roads tonight.

110
00:05:32,019 --> 00:05:33,505
The span came down.

111
00:05:33,805 --> 00:05:35,245
Eight people had been on it.

112
00:05:35,545 --> 00:05:36,799
Six of them died.

113
00:05:37,099 --> 00:05:40,118
Which raises the first thing that does not fit.

114
00:05:40,418 --> 00:05:45,631
If a ship losing power is a known event, and this bridge had protection built for

115
00:05:45,631 --> 00:05:48,591
exactly that, why did the protection not hold?

116
00:05:50,391 --> 00:05:52,295
Everybody gets this part wrong.

117
00:05:52,595 --> 00:05:55,799
The obvious answer is that a ship lost power and hit a bridge.

118
00:05:56,099 --> 00:05:59,907
That is true, and it explains nothing, because ships lose power.

119
00:06:00,207 --> 00:06:01,600
It is not rare.

120
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:06,440
A vessel is a small industrial city with four generators and several thousand

121
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:07,148
connections.

122
00:06:07,448 --> 00:06:09,491
Industrial cities have bad nights.

123
00:06:09,791 --> 00:06:12,995
So the question was never whether the Dali could lose power.

124
00:06:13,295 --> 00:06:16,453
A ship of this class is built on the assumption that she will.

125
00:06:16,753 --> 00:06:19,911
The whole architecture exists to make a blackout survivable.

126
00:06:20,211 --> 00:06:25,197
Two step-down transformers, TR1 and TR2, either of which can feed the low-voltage

127
00:06:25,197 --> 00:06:25,505
side.

128
00:06:25,805 --> 00:06:28,452
A breaker on each side of each transformer.

129
00:06:28,752 --> 00:06:33,058
A bus tie that can be closed to run the two halves as one, which is how she was

130
00:06:33,058 --> 00:06:34,093
running that night.

131
00:06:34,393 --> 00:06:39,014
And critically: the ship's critical systems were originally designed with reliable

132
00:06:39,014 --> 00:06:43,691
redundancies and automatic restart capabilities, so that after a blackout she could

133
00:06:43,691 --> 00:06:45,213
get her power back quickly.

134
00:06:45,513 --> 00:06:47,510
That is not an opinion.

135
00:06:47,810 --> 00:06:50,921
That is what the United States told a grand jury this year.

136
00:06:51,221 --> 00:06:54,797
Now look at what the humans on the water actually did in those four minutes.

137
00:06:55,097 --> 00:06:59,970
The engineers found the breakers and closed them by hand — twice, under a blackout,

138
00:06:59,970 --> 00:07:01,320
at night, in a channel.

139
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:05,707
The pilots called for a tug within seventy-four seconds of the first blackout.

140
00:07:06,007 --> 00:07:08,515
The pilots' dispatcher called the Coast Guard.

141
00:07:08,815 --> 00:07:12,855
A pilot broadcast a warning to everything floating in the Patapsco.

142
00:07:13,155 --> 00:07:15,198
The duty officer stopped the traffic.

143
00:07:15,498 --> 00:07:17,356
The tug turned around and ran.

144
00:07:17,656 --> 00:07:22,354
Every one of those is a person doing exactly the thing their training says to do, in

145
00:07:22,354 --> 00:07:25,319
the right order, faster than most of us would manage.

146
00:07:25,619 --> 00:07:27,152
The tug was three miles out.

147
00:07:27,452 --> 00:07:29,728
The bridge was two tenths of a mile ahead.

148
00:07:30,028 --> 00:07:32,025
Nobody was going to close that gap.

149
00:07:32,325 --> 00:07:35,529
And the second wrong answer usually arrives right here.

150
00:07:35,829 --> 00:07:40,724
If the tug could not get there in time, then surely the mistake was letting the tugs

151
00:07:40,724 --> 00:07:42,006
go in the first place.

152
00:07:42,306 --> 00:07:43,374
It was not.

153
00:07:43,674 --> 00:07:45,253
That is the procedure.

154
00:07:45,553 --> 00:07:50,106
Tugs escort a ship out of the berth and through the tight water, and once she is in

155
00:07:50,106 --> 00:07:53,726
the channel and making way under her own power, they are released.

156
00:07:54,026 --> 00:07:59,046
About seven minutes past one, in the channel, that is what happened, and the Safety

157
00:07:59,046 --> 00:08:01,224
Board records it as normal practice.

158
00:08:01,524 --> 00:08:04,728
Every ship leaving Baltimore that week did the same thing.

159
00:08:05,028 --> 00:08:08,511
Every ship leaving most ports in the world does the same thing tonight.

160
00:08:08,811 --> 00:08:13,463
A rule that only looks wrong once, in hindsight, after a wire fails, is not the

161
00:08:13,463 --> 00:08:14,523
thing that failed.

162
00:08:14,823 --> 00:08:18,678
So walk the four minutes again and ask what lever was actually available.

163
00:08:18,978 --> 00:08:23,041
The emergency generator started and picked up the emergency bus, exactly as

164
00:08:23,041 --> 00:08:23,529
designed.

165
00:08:23,829 --> 00:08:26,383
The engineers found and closed the breakers by hand.

166
00:08:26,683 --> 00:08:27,472
Twice.

167
00:08:27,772 --> 00:08:28,747
In the dark.

168
00:08:29,047 --> 00:08:32,902
The pilots called for help, warned the water, and told the Coast Guard.

169
00:08:33,202 --> 00:08:34,688
The shore stopped the cars.

170
00:08:34,988 --> 00:08:38,657
And every one of those levers moves something other than the ship.

171
00:08:38,957 --> 00:08:43,590
Because the one thing that would have changed the outcome was the propeller, and the

172
00:08:43,590 --> 00:08:47,781
propeller was downstream of the exact system that kept switching itself off.

173
00:08:48,081 --> 00:08:52,797
The main engine was a separate machine from the four generators — but the pumps that

174
00:08:52,797 --> 00:08:57,457
cooled it, and the pumps that drove the steering, lived on the low-voltage bus that

175
00:08:57,457 --> 00:08:58,019
went dark.

176
00:08:58,319 --> 00:08:59,898
That is the shape of the trap.

177
00:09:00,198 --> 00:09:02,752
The ship was not disabled because her engine broke.

178
00:09:03,052 --> 00:09:04,631
Her engine did not break.

179
00:09:04,931 --> 00:09:09,531
She was disabled because the small electrical support around a very large engine

180
00:09:09,531 --> 00:09:14,246
went away, and a very large engine without cooling and without steering assistance

181
00:09:14,246 --> 00:09:15,798
is not a propulsion system.

182
00:09:16,098 --> 00:09:18,281
It is ballast with a crankshaft in it.

183
00:09:18,581 --> 00:09:22,864
There is one more detail in the record that makes this concrete, and it is the sort

184
00:09:22,864 --> 00:09:25,083
of thing only an investigator would notice.

185
00:09:25,383 --> 00:09:28,262
The ship's data recorder never stopped listening.

186
00:09:28,562 --> 00:09:33,842
International rules require a vessel of her tonnage, built after the middle of 2014

187
00:09:33,842 --> 00:09:39,186
and trading internationally, to carry one — and through both blackouts, with most of

188
00:09:39,186 --> 00:09:42,494
the ship dark, the bridge audio kept being captured.

189
00:09:42,794 --> 00:09:46,649
Which means we are not reconstructing this from memory or from lawyers.

190
00:09:46,949 --> 00:09:51,051
There is a recording of four minutes in which a professional crew did the right

191
00:09:51,051 --> 00:09:53,543
things in the right order and it did not matter.

192
00:09:53,843 --> 00:09:58,843
That is a rare and horrible kind of evidence, and it forecloses the comfortable

193
00:09:58,843 --> 00:09:59,602
explanation.

194
00:09:59,902 --> 00:10:04,235
There is no moment on that tape where somebody freezes, or argues, or fails to

195
00:10:04,235 --> 00:10:05,846
understand what is happening.

196
00:10:06,146 --> 00:10:10,093
What there is, instead, is a ship that kept coming back to life from the neck up.

197
00:10:10,393 --> 00:10:15,040
At twenty-seven minutes and thirty-two seconds past one she had her lights, her

198
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:17,452
low-voltage bus and generator number two.

199
00:10:17,752 --> 00:10:22,117
Ninety seconds earlier she had had all of that too, and then lost it again.

200
00:10:22,417 --> 00:10:26,908
Twice in four minutes, the thing that was designed never to happen twice in a row,

201
00:10:26,908 --> 00:10:28,222
happened twice in a row.

202
00:10:28,522 --> 00:10:31,355
So the story is not a story about people failing.

203
00:10:31,655 --> 00:10:35,961
It is a story about a machine that was supposed to come back and did not come back

204
00:10:35,961 --> 00:10:40,372
in time — and about the difference between getting your lights back and getting your

205
00:10:40,372 --> 00:10:40,897
ship back.

206
00:10:41,197 --> 00:10:45,980
At twenty-seven minutes and thirty-two seconds past one, the Dali had electricity.

207
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:47,487
She had lighting.

208
00:10:47,787 --> 00:10:51,409
She had her low-voltage bus, fed from generator number two.

209
00:10:51,709 --> 00:10:56,060
What she did not have was a turning propeller, and without a turning propeller a

210
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:57,746
rudder is a piece of furniture.

211
00:10:58,046 --> 00:11:01,111
Water has to move across it for it to do anything at all.

212
00:11:01,411 --> 00:11:06,621
She was, at that moment, nine hundred and forty-seven feet of loaded steel with the

213
00:11:06,621 --> 00:11:10,513
lights on, going where the tide and her own momentum took her.

214
00:11:10,813 --> 00:11:15,423
Which leaves one question, and it is the question two federal investigations spent

215
00:11:15,423 --> 00:11:16,154
two years on.

216
00:11:16,454 --> 00:11:18,451
Why did the power keep going out?

217
00:11:20,251 --> 00:11:22,527
Before the failures, the reasons.

218
00:11:22,827 --> 00:11:27,239
This is a system that was designed by people who had thought about this exact night.

219
00:11:27,539 --> 00:11:32,118
A vessel like the Dali distributes power on a high-voltage bus fed by four diesel

220
00:11:32,118 --> 00:11:32,740
generators.

221
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:37,634
A four-hundred-and-forty-volt low-voltage bus hangs off it through two step-down

222
00:11:37,634 --> 00:11:38,381
transformers.

223
00:11:38,681 --> 00:11:43,799
The low-voltage side is where the ordinary life of the ship lives: the lighting, the

224
00:11:43,799 --> 00:11:47,272
steering gear pumps, the main engine cooling water pumps.

225
00:11:47,572 --> 00:11:50,405
The redundancy is deliberate and it is doubled.

226
00:11:50,705 --> 00:11:53,027
Two transformers, so one can fail.

227
00:11:53,327 --> 00:11:56,438
Breakers either side of each, so a fault can be isolated.

228
00:11:56,738 --> 00:12:01,140
Generators that restart on their own, so a blackout does not need a human in the

229
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:01,800
loop to end.

230
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:03,725
That is the design.

231
00:12:04,025 --> 00:12:05,093
Now the night.

232
00:12:05,393 --> 00:12:09,636
Ten hours before the Dali sailed, while she was still tied up in Baltimore, the

233
00:12:09,636 --> 00:12:11,570
lights went out on her once already.

234
00:12:11,870 --> 00:12:15,914
A crewmember was working on the exhaust scrubber system for the engine driving the

235
00:12:15,914 --> 00:12:17,443
only generator that was online.

236
00:12:17,743 --> 00:12:20,297
He closed an inline exhaust damper by mistake.

237
00:12:20,597 --> 00:12:22,362
The exhaust had nowhere to go.

238
00:12:22,662 --> 00:12:25,681
The generator stopped, and the ship went dark at the pier.

239
00:12:25,981 --> 00:12:30,772
Then it happened a second time in port, for a different reason — not enough fuel

240
00:12:30,772 --> 00:12:32,808
pressure for the online generator.

241
00:12:33,108 --> 00:12:34,919
Nobody died in either of those.

242
00:12:35,219 --> 00:12:39,538
They are the kind of thing that gets written in a log and talked about over a meal.

243
00:12:39,838 --> 00:12:44,027
So why would a night at a pier where nothing happened turn out to matter more than

244
00:12:44,027 --> 00:12:45,457
anything else in this story?

245
00:12:45,757 --> 00:12:48,915
Because of what was done to put the ship right afterwards.

246
00:12:49,215 --> 00:12:53,805
Something happened during the recovery from the second in-port blackout, and it is

247
00:12:53,805 --> 00:12:55,484
the hinge of the entire night.

248
00:12:55,784 --> 00:12:59,081
Coming back up, the crew changed the ship's electrical configuration.

249
00:12:59,381 --> 00:13:05,543
They stopped using breakers HR2 and LR2 with transformer TR2 — which had been in use

250
00:13:05,543 --> 00:13:09,505
for several months — and switched to HR1, LR1 and TR1.

251
00:13:09,805 --> 00:13:13,056
And that is the configuration the Dali sailed on the next morning.

252
00:13:13,356 --> 00:13:18,790
Ten hours later, six tenths of a mile from the Key Bridge, it was HR1 and LR1 that

253
00:13:18,790 --> 00:13:19,254
opened.

254
00:13:19,554 --> 00:13:23,176
It took the National Transportation Safety Board twenty months to say why.

255
00:13:23,476 --> 00:13:28,263
On the eighteenth of November, 2025, the Board met and determined the probable

256
00:13:28,263 --> 00:13:28,631
cause.

257
00:13:28,931 --> 00:13:30,278
It was a wire.

258
00:13:30,578 --> 00:13:35,352
Specifically: a loose signal wire connection to a terminal block, stemming from the

259
00:13:35,352 --> 00:13:39,953
improper installation of wire-label banding, which caused the loss of electrical

260
00:13:39,953 --> 00:13:44,324
power, which caused the loss of propulsion and steering close to the bridge.

261
00:13:44,624 --> 00:13:47,735
The mechanism is small enough to hold between two fingers.

262
00:13:48,035 --> 00:13:51,379
A terminal block has a spring-clamp gate that grips a wire.

263
00:13:51,679 --> 00:13:54,373
Somebody had put a band of labelling around that wire.

264
00:13:54,673 --> 00:13:57,413
The band stopped the wire seating fully into the gate.

265
00:13:57,713 --> 00:13:59,338
The connection was inadequate.

266
00:13:59,638 --> 00:14:01,031
That is the whole thing.

267
00:14:01,331 --> 00:14:06,596
A ship of this size, a bridge of this size, six men, and the physical cause is a

268
00:14:06,596 --> 00:14:11,269
label that was a fraction of a millimetre too thick in the wrong place.

269
00:14:11,569 --> 00:14:13,519
But that explains the first blackout.

270
00:14:13,819 --> 00:14:16,559
It does not explain why she did not come back.

271
00:14:16,859 --> 00:14:19,227
Because remember: she was built to come back.

272
00:14:19,527 --> 00:14:20,920
Automatic restart.

273
00:14:21,220 --> 00:14:22,753
Reliable redundancies.

274
00:14:23,053 --> 00:14:25,096
That was the promise in the drawings.

275
00:14:25,396 --> 00:14:30,842
On the twelfth of May, 2026, a federal court in Maryland unsealed an indictment, and

276
00:14:30,842 --> 00:14:35,381
the United States offered its account of why the promise was not kept.

277
00:14:35,681 --> 00:14:40,371
Before a word of it: an indictment is merely an accusation, and all defendants are

278
00:14:40,371 --> 00:14:45,062
presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law.

279
00:14:45,362 --> 00:14:49,927
Those are the Department of Justice's own words, printed underneath its own press

280
00:14:49,927 --> 00:14:50,378
release.

281
00:14:50,678 --> 00:14:52,675
This programme will hold to them.

282
00:14:52,975 --> 00:14:58,720
The three defendants are Synergy Marine, of Singapore; Synergy Maritime, of Chennai,

283
00:14:58,720 --> 00:15:04,465
India; and Radhakrishnan Karthik Nair, forty-seven years old, an Indian national who

284
00:15:04,465 --> 00:15:09,322
worked for both companies as the technical superintendent for the Dali.

285
00:15:09,622 --> 00:15:13,939
They are charged with conspiracy to defraud the United States, with willfully

286
00:15:13,939 --> 00:15:18,536
failing to immediately inform the Coast Guard of a known hazardous condition, with

287
00:15:18,536 --> 00:15:22,068
obstruction of an agency proceeding, and with false statements.

288
00:15:22,368 --> 00:15:27,473
The two companies additionally face misdemeanour environmental counts for what went

289
00:15:27,473 --> 00:15:31,470
into the Patapsco River — containers, oil, and the bridge itself.

290
00:15:31,770 --> 00:15:34,046
The allegation about the machinery is this.

291
00:15:34,346 --> 00:15:38,975
The government says the Dali lost power twice in a four-minute span, and that a

292
00:15:38,975 --> 00:15:43,077
loose wire in a high-voltage switchboard likely caused the first loss.

293
00:15:43,377 --> 00:15:48,078
And then, the government says, the ship did not recover the way she was designed to

294
00:15:48,078 --> 00:15:50,854
recover — because the defendants had altered her.

295
00:15:51,154 --> 00:15:55,863
According to the indictment, they relied on a flushing pump to supply fuel to two of

296
00:15:55,863 --> 00:15:57,377
the Dali's four generators.

297
00:15:57,677 --> 00:16:00,185
A flushing pump is a housekeeping device.

298
00:16:00,485 --> 00:16:04,340
It is for moving fuel around, not for feeding an engine in anger.

299
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:09,400
And the crucial property, in the government's telling, is that it was not designed

300
00:16:09,400 --> 00:16:11,838
to automatically restart after a blackout.

301
00:16:12,138 --> 00:16:17,082
So the chain the indictment draws is: the lights go out, the generators need fuel,

302
00:16:17,082 --> 00:16:22,147
the pump that was feeding them cannot start itself, and the ship blacks out a second

303
00:16:22,147 --> 00:16:22,448
time.

304
00:16:22,748 --> 00:16:26,510
And then comes the sentence that will be fought over in court for years.

305
00:16:26,810 --> 00:16:31,319
The indictment alleges that if the Dali had used the proper fuel supply pumps, the

306
00:16:31,319 --> 00:16:35,773
vessel would have regained power in time to navigate safely under the Key Bridge.

307
00:16:36,073 --> 00:16:37,002
Alleges.

308
00:16:37,302 --> 00:16:38,324
Not found.

309
00:16:38,624 --> 00:16:39,785
Not proven.

310
00:16:40,085 --> 00:16:45,210
A grand jury heard a case and returned a charge, and that is a beginning, not an

311
00:16:45,210 --> 00:16:45,658
ending.

312
00:16:45,958 --> 00:16:50,648
And then, five weeks later, something happened that is not an allegation at all.

313
00:16:50,948 --> 00:16:55,503
On the eighteenth of June, 2026, the chief engineer of the Dali entered into a

314
00:16:55,503 --> 00:16:58,657
deferred prosecution agreement with the United States.

315
00:16:58,957 --> 00:17:01,418
His name is Karthikeyan Deenadayalan.

316
00:17:01,718 --> 00:17:03,158
He was aboard that night.

317
00:17:03,458 --> 00:17:07,386
In that agreement he admitted to failing to report a hazardous condition, in

318
00:17:07,386 --> 00:17:09,867
violation of the Ports and Waterways Safety Act.

319
00:17:10,167 --> 00:17:12,721
And the things he acknowledged are worth hearing slowly.

320
00:17:13,021 --> 00:17:18,028
He had also been chief engineer on two of the Dali's sister ships — the Maersk

321
00:17:18,028 --> 00:17:19,569
Saltoro and the Cezanne.

322
00:17:19,869 --> 00:17:23,491
And he knew that all three of them used an unsafe fuel supply pump.

323
00:17:23,791 --> 00:17:24,906
Not one ship.

324
00:17:25,206 --> 00:17:25,949
Three.

325
00:17:26,249 --> 00:17:30,392
He acknowledged that the pump lacked redundancy, and that this compromised the

326
00:17:30,392 --> 00:17:34,376
vessels' safe navigation and their ability to recover from a loss of power.

327
00:17:34,676 --> 00:17:39,260
And he knew that a ship unable to recover from a loss of power could adversely

328
00:17:39,260 --> 00:17:44,196
affect the safety of the vessel itself — and of any bridge, structure or shore area.

329
00:17:44,496 --> 00:17:47,700
That sentence was written in a legal agreement in 2026.

330
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:53,062
It describes, in the abstract, exactly what happened in the Patapsco in 2024.

331
00:17:53,362 --> 00:17:58,164
He also said he had spoken and corresponded with people at Synergy about using that

332
00:17:58,164 --> 00:18:00,421
pump, including the co-defendant, Nair.

333
00:18:00,721 --> 00:18:05,131
And he said something else, and here the guardrail goes back up, because this is one

334
00:18:05,131 --> 00:18:09,173
man's account of another man's conduct and that other man has not been tried.

335
00:18:09,473 --> 00:18:13,673
The chief engineer said that Nair directed him to send a convincing email to the

336
00:18:13,673 --> 00:18:17,821
ship's charterer, so that the charterer would not ask more questions about fuel

337
00:18:17,821 --> 00:18:21,129
consumption and find out that the flushing pump was being used.

338
00:18:21,429 --> 00:18:25,548
There is one more count, and it is the one that tells you what kind of story this

339
00:18:25,548 --> 00:18:25,701
is.

340
00:18:26,001 --> 00:18:30,539
The obstruction charge relates, among other things, to what Nair is alleged to have

341
00:18:30,539 --> 00:18:35,077
told the investigators: that he was unaware the Dali was using the flushing pump to

342
00:18:35,077 --> 00:18:36,171
fuel the generators.

343
00:18:36,471 --> 00:18:38,143
An alteration to a ship.

344
00:18:38,443 --> 00:18:39,558
In an engine room.

345
00:18:39,858 --> 00:18:41,158
Below the waterline.

346
00:18:41,458 --> 00:18:45,591
Which, the United States says, the investigators had to find rather than be told.

347
00:18:45,891 --> 00:18:47,795
And what about the bridge in all this?

348
00:18:48,095 --> 00:18:53,210
The Board looked at that too, and its finding is quieter and, for anyone who drives,

349
00:18:53,210 --> 00:18:53,575
worse.

350
00:18:53,875 --> 00:18:57,470
Contributing to the collapse and to the loss of life was the absence of

351
00:18:57,470 --> 00:19:01,217
countermeasures to reduce the bridge's vulnerability to being struck by an

352
00:19:01,217 --> 00:19:05,116
ocean-going vessel — countermeasures that could have been put in place if the

353
00:19:05,116 --> 00:19:09,014
Maryland Transportation Authority had carried out a vulnerability assessment.

354
00:19:09,314 --> 00:19:12,658
The guidance recommending those assessments is longstanding.

355
00:19:12,958 --> 00:19:16,834
It comes from the American Association of State Highway and Transportation

356
00:19:16,834 --> 00:19:19,506
Officials, and it has been sitting there for years.

357
00:19:19,806 --> 00:19:23,382
And here the Board did something careful that is worth noticing.

358
00:19:23,682 --> 00:19:26,097
It did not find that Maryland knew and ignored it.

359
00:19:26,397 --> 00:19:29,741
It found that they were likely unaware of the potential risk.

360
00:19:30,041 --> 00:19:31,156
Not a cover-up.

361
00:19:31,456 --> 00:19:36,197
Something more ordinary and harder to fix: a recommendation that existed, and a

362
00:19:36,197 --> 00:19:39,258
bridge whose owner did not know it applied to them.

363
00:19:39,558 --> 00:19:42,484
A vulnerability assessment sounds like a filing cabinet.

364
00:19:42,784 --> 00:19:43,991
It is a sum.

365
00:19:44,291 --> 00:19:45,731
Take the bridge you have.

366
00:19:46,031 --> 00:19:50,342
Take the ships that use the channel now, not the ships that used it when the bridge

367
00:19:50,342 --> 00:19:50,861
was drawn.

368
00:19:51,161 --> 00:19:55,526
Work out what happens to that pier if that ship arrives at that speed at that angle.

369
00:19:55,826 --> 00:19:58,937
Then find out whether the protection you already have is protection.

370
00:19:59,237 --> 00:20:00,491
Or decoration.

371
00:20:00,791 --> 00:20:03,252
The dolphins by pier seventeen were real.

372
00:20:03,552 --> 00:20:07,128
Sheet pile and concrete, built by people who were not careless.

373
00:20:07,428 --> 00:20:09,704
But protection is never absolute.

374
00:20:10,004 --> 00:20:14,230
It is protection against something, at some speed, of some size.

375
00:20:14,530 --> 00:20:18,805
And a bridge stays exactly where it is while the ships underneath it keep getting

376
00:20:18,805 --> 00:20:19,174
bigger.

377
00:20:19,474 --> 00:20:20,960
That is the failure.

378
00:20:21,260 --> 00:20:22,421
Not neglect.

379
00:20:22,721 --> 00:20:25,229
Arithmetic that went quietly out of date.

380
00:20:25,529 --> 00:20:30,322
So how many other bridges are standing over shipping channels tonight without anyone

381
00:20:30,322 --> 00:20:31,520
having done that sum?

382
00:20:33,320 --> 00:20:36,942
The Board answered that question on the twentieth of March, 2025.

383
00:20:37,242 --> 00:20:38,264
Sixty-eight.

384
00:20:38,564 --> 00:20:42,888
Sixty-eight bridges in the United States for which the NTSB recommended a

385
00:20:42,888 --> 00:20:44,369
vulnerability evaluation.

386
00:20:44,669 --> 00:20:47,270
Not sixty-eight bridges that are unsafe.

387
00:20:47,570 --> 00:20:52,234
Sixty-eight bridges where nobody had done the arithmetic — where the owner could not

388
00:20:52,234 --> 00:20:56,732
say what would happen if a modern ship hit a pier, because the question had never

389
00:20:56,732 --> 00:20:58,065
been asked in that form.

390
00:20:58,365 --> 00:21:00,501
Every one of those is somebody's commute.

391
00:21:00,801 --> 00:21:04,888
Meanwhile, the money moved through a different courthouse, and it moved fast.

392
00:21:05,188 --> 00:21:10,499
Six days after the collapse, on the first of April, 2024, the ship's owner and

393
00:21:10,499 --> 00:21:16,150
manager — Grace Ocean Private Limited and Synergy Marine Private Limited — filed in

394
00:21:16,150 --> 00:21:21,256
the District of Maryland for exoneration from, or limitation of, liability.

395
00:21:21,556 --> 00:21:26,217
That is a very old piece of American maritime law, and it does something that sounds

396
00:21:26,217 --> 00:21:28,104
impossible when you first hear it.

397
00:21:28,404 --> 00:21:32,901
It allows the owner of a vessel to ask a court to cap what they can be made to pay,

398
00:21:32,901 --> 00:21:35,556
at roughly the value of what is left of the ship.

399
00:21:35,856 --> 00:21:39,060
Before the outrage, the reason.

400
00:21:39,360 --> 00:21:41,821
That law exists because of a hard problem.

401
00:21:42,121 --> 00:21:44,304
A ship is an asset that leaves.

402
00:21:44,604 --> 00:21:49,767
She sails out of reach, under people her owner cannot see, into weather nobody

403
00:21:49,767 --> 00:21:50,363
controls.

404
00:21:50,663 --> 00:21:56,209
If one voyage could destroy the company that financed it, the argument runs, nobody

405
00:21:56,209 --> 00:21:57,211
finances ships.

406
00:21:57,511 --> 00:21:59,276
So the law strikes a deal.

407
00:21:59,576 --> 00:22:03,716
An owner who did not know about the fault, and could not have known, may ask a court

408
00:22:03,716 --> 00:22:06,821
to cap what he owes at the value of the vessel and her freight.

409
00:22:07,121 --> 00:22:10,372
In the abstract it is a sensible rule for a trading nation.

410
00:22:10,672 --> 00:22:13,830
After a bridge falls on a work crew it is a number.

411
00:22:14,130 --> 00:22:18,521
The figure they asked to be limited to was about forty-three point seven million

412
00:22:18,521 --> 00:22:18,960
dollars.

413
00:22:19,260 --> 00:22:24,311
Against that, the United States put in a claim of one hundred and three million,

414
00:22:24,311 --> 00:22:29,552
seventy-eight thousand and fifty-six dollars, under the Rivers and Harbors Act, the

415
00:22:29,552 --> 00:22:34,856
Oil Pollution Act, and general maritime law — for what it had cost the government to

416
00:22:34,856 --> 00:22:36,118
answer the collapse.

417
00:22:36,418 --> 00:22:40,784
The companies agreed to pay one hundred and one million, nine hundred and eighty

418
00:22:40,784 --> 00:22:41,712
thousand dollars.

419
00:22:42,012 --> 00:22:44,241
That is a settlement of a civil claim.

420
00:22:44,541 --> 00:22:49,013
It is not an admission of anything, and it is not a criminal finding, and this

421
00:22:49,013 --> 00:22:51,879
programme is not going to let it stand in for one.

422
00:22:52,179 --> 00:22:56,991
Then, in May of 2026, the criminal case arrived, and with it a number that resets

423
00:22:56,991 --> 00:22:58,773
the scale of everything above.

424
00:22:59,073 --> 00:23:03,032
The indictment alleges the economic loss in this case is at least five billion

425
00:23:03,032 --> 00:23:03,438
dollars.

426
00:23:03,738 --> 00:23:08,336
Five billion, against a request to be capped at forty-three point seven million.

427
00:23:08,636 --> 00:23:13,657
The gap between those two numbers is the entire argument, and it will be argued by

428
00:23:13,657 --> 00:23:18,249
real lawyers in a real courtroom in Maryland, and it is not finished today.

429
00:23:18,549 --> 00:23:23,292
The investigation that produced the charges ran for two years and involved the FBI,

430
00:23:23,292 --> 00:23:27,635
the Coast Guard Investigative Service, and the criminal investigators of the

431
00:23:27,635 --> 00:23:32,435
Environmental Protection Agency, some of whom were among the first people aboard the

432
00:23:32,435 --> 00:23:33,178
wrecked ship.

433
00:23:33,478 --> 00:23:37,761
It is worth saying plainly where that leaves things on the day this programme is

434
00:23:37,761 --> 00:23:38,029
made.

435
00:23:38,329 --> 00:23:39,908
The Safety Board has finished.

436
00:23:40,208 --> 00:23:45,299
It met, it voted, it named a probable cause, and it said the complete report would

437
00:23:45,299 --> 00:23:45,734
follow.

438
00:23:46,034 --> 00:23:50,604
The civil claim by the United States is resolved, by agreement, for a hundred and

439
00:23:50,604 --> 00:23:53,650
one million, nine hundred and eighty thousand dollars.

440
00:23:53,950 --> 00:23:56,226
And the criminal case has barely begun.

441
00:23:56,526 --> 00:23:58,894
An indictment was unsealed in May.

442
00:23:59,194 --> 00:24:02,863
Three defendants stand charged — two companies and one man.

443
00:24:03,163 --> 00:24:04,788
None of them has been tried.

444
00:24:05,088 --> 00:24:07,410
None of them has been convicted of anything.

445
00:24:07,710 --> 00:24:11,769
The presumption of innocence is not a formality that a documentary gets to skip

446
00:24:11,769 --> 00:24:13,979
because the facts underneath are upsetting.

447
00:24:14,279 --> 00:24:18,695
There is also a distinction in that indictment which is easy to miss and which will

448
00:24:18,695 --> 00:24:19,759
decide a great deal.

449
00:24:20,059 --> 00:24:23,403
The counts are not, in the main, about building a bad ship.

450
00:24:23,703 --> 00:24:28,301
They are about what was done with a good one, and about what was said afterwards.

451
00:24:28,601 --> 00:24:29,716
Conspiracy.

452
00:24:30,016 --> 00:24:33,685
Failing to tell the Coast Guard about a known hazardous condition.

453
00:24:33,985 --> 00:24:35,935
Obstruction of an agency proceeding.

454
00:24:36,235 --> 00:24:37,628
False statements.

455
00:24:37,928 --> 00:24:42,655
Read them in order and they describe a sequence: something was changed, the change

456
00:24:42,655 --> 00:24:47,440
was not reported, and when investigators came asking, the government says they were

457
00:24:47,440 --> 00:24:47,959
not told.

458
00:24:48,259 --> 00:24:53,469
Whether that sequence is true is not for this programme to decide, and it is not

459
00:24:53,469 --> 00:24:54,250
decided yet.

460
00:24:54,550 --> 00:24:58,730
It is for twelve people in a courtroom in Maryland who have not yet been chosen.

461
00:24:59,030 --> 00:25:03,344
And all of it — the Board's twenty months, the grand jury's two years, the

462
00:25:03,344 --> 00:25:08,183
settlement, the sixty-eight bridges — all of it comes back to a stretch of time you

463
00:25:08,183 --> 00:25:09,990
could hold your breath through.

464
00:25:10,290 --> 00:25:14,470
The duty officer's order went out at twenty-seven minutes and fifty-three seconds

465
00:25:14,470 --> 00:25:14,934
past one.

466
00:25:15,234 --> 00:25:18,438
The ship touched the pier at about twenty-nine minutes past one.

467
00:25:18,738 --> 00:25:20,131
Sixty-seven seconds.

468
00:25:20,431 --> 00:25:25,315
And in that window a decision made ten hours earlier at a pier, and a modification

469
00:25:25,315 --> 00:25:30,139
made at some point before that in an engine room, and a band of label on a single

470
00:25:30,139 --> 00:25:33,295
wire, all arrived at the same place at the same time.

471
00:25:33,595 --> 00:25:36,939
So what is the rule that was actually running that night?

472
00:25:38,739 --> 00:25:39,900
Go back to the deck.

473
00:25:40,200 --> 00:25:42,058
The work light is still burning.

474
00:25:42,358 --> 00:25:44,308
The cone is still standing in it.

475
00:25:44,608 --> 00:25:46,094
Nothing has happened yet.

476
00:25:46,394 --> 00:25:49,784
Down the channel, a ship is leaving a berth with her lights on.

477
00:25:50,084 --> 00:25:52,034
Every lamp aboard her is lit.

478
00:25:52,334 --> 00:25:54,563
That is what a working ship looks like.

479
00:25:55,163 --> 00:25:57,299
She loses them at twenty-five past one.

480
00:25:57,599 --> 00:25:58,760
She gets them back.

481
00:25:59,060 --> 00:26:00,453
She loses them again.

482
00:26:00,753 --> 00:26:05,663
And at twenty-seven minutes and thirty-two seconds past one, with two tenths of a

483
00:26:05,663 --> 00:26:08,694
mile to run, she gets them back for the last time.

484
00:26:08,994 --> 00:26:10,434
Lit from bow to stern.

485
00:26:10,734 --> 00:26:12,127
Every lamp burning.

486
00:26:12,427 --> 00:26:14,470
And not moving under her own power.

487
00:26:15,070 --> 00:26:17,067
The drawings promised she could recover.

488
00:26:17,367 --> 00:26:18,853
They were honest drawings.

489
00:26:19,153 --> 00:26:24,373
Somewhere between those drawings and that water, the United States says, the promise

490
00:26:24,373 --> 00:26:27,605
was edited — and the edit stayed in the engine room.

491
00:26:27,905 --> 00:26:29,623
Nobody has been tried for that.

492
00:26:29,923 --> 00:26:33,983
On the other side of the channel, the guidance about ship strikes had been written,

493
00:26:33,983 --> 00:26:37,261
and published, and had not reached the people who owned the bridge.

494
00:26:37,561 --> 00:26:39,837
The Board found they were likely unaware.

495
00:26:40,137 --> 00:26:41,205
Not a villain.

496
00:26:41,505 --> 00:26:42,434
A gap.

497
00:26:42,734 --> 00:26:48,171
Sixty-eight bridges are now on a list that says: go and check what happens if a ship

498
00:26:48,171 --> 00:26:48,818
hits this.

499
00:26:49,118 --> 00:26:52,973
That list did not exist on the twenty-fifth of March, 2024.

500
00:26:53,573 --> 00:26:55,895
The work light on the deck goes off at dawn.

501
00:26:56,195 --> 00:26:58,192
The cone gets stacked on the truck.

502
00:26:58,492 --> 00:26:59,607
The lane opens.

503
00:26:59,907 --> 00:27:02,136
Every light in this story came back on.

504
00:27:02,436 --> 00:27:04,758
That was never the thing that had to hold.
